.TH PRELUDE-HOOKS 5 2026-10-19 "Peios prelude 0.0.7"
.SH NAME
prelude-hooks \- runtime hook sequences consumed by Prelude
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /system/prelude/hooks.seq.1
.br
.I /system/prelude/hooks.seq.2
.SH DESCRIPTION
These files describe boot hooks selected and ordered by mkirf.
Prelude 0.0.7 reads the highest available version it supports, through
version 2. The first line must match the selected version.
.PP
Version 1 begins with "hookseq 1". Each subsequent nonempty line names a
hook executable. Version 2 begins with "hookseq 2" and contains stanzas:
.PP
.nf
hook /usr/libexec/prelude/hooks.d/mount-root
provides root-mounted
requires devices-ready
after storage-probed
.fi
.PP
A hook line starts a stanza. Its path must be nonempty. Subsequent provides,
contributes, requires, and after lines contain whitespace-separated
capability names. Repeated declarations append names. Empty lines are
ignored. Unknown keys or a declaration before any hook are errors.
Every required capability must have a provider or contributor in the sequence.
An after declaration can name an absent capability.
.PP
A capability settles when all its providers and contributors have completed.
It is achieved when settled and at least one provider succeeded, if providers
exist. Contributors that decline count as complete. Requires waits for
achievement; after waits only for settlement.
.PP
Hooks execute sequentially with direct console output. Exit 0 satisfies the
hook, 69 declines it, and 75 defers it without completing it. A deferring hook
promises that it has made no changes. Other exits and signal termination fail
boot. Stopped hooks are killed, reaped, and reported as failures.
.PP
A deferred hook is retried on another pass after progress. A full pass with
pending work and no completion fails instead of waiting indefinitely.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /hooks.seq
Legacy version 1 sequence. Its marker is "hookseq 1".
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/prelude/hook-log.sh
Sourced POSIX shell helper for console messages. Call hook_log_init with the
component name, then use log, log_ok, log_skip, log_warn, or log_fail.
Warnings and failures go to standard error; other messages go to standard
output. Component names and messages are printed literally, including
backslashes, regardless of color. TERM=dumb in /proc/cmdline disables color. The helper requires
prelude-hook-abi level 3; it does not implement Prelude's quiet setting.
.SH NOTES
This is the runtime sequence format, not the source metadata block parsed by
mkirf. The writer and reader are independently versioned. Changes to their
shared format require coordinated compatibility review.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkirf (1),
.BR prelude (8)
